Booting this kernel on a real Raspberry Pi 3 Model B
=====================================================

On a Pi 3 the ARM cores do not fetch the kernel directly.  The VideoCore
GPU boots first, reads the firmware and config from the first (FAT) SD
partition, loads the ARM kernel image into RAM at 0x80000, and releases
core 0 into it.  So there is no ELF loader on the board: the kernel must
be a raw flat binary, and its startup must cope with the EL the firmware
drops it at.

Build
-----
Run these commands from the repository root.  Set ROOT explicitly before
changing directory; setting it to $PWD after the cd points the build at the
board directory and makes 7a and ndate appear to be missing.

    export ROOT=$PWD
    export PATH=$ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/bin:$PATH
    cd os/inferno/raspi3b
    mk INIT=raspi3binit boot

kernel8.img is iraspi3b (ELF) reduced to its loadable segments laid out
at their link addresses, produced by mkrawimg.py from the ELF program
headers.  (7l -H6 emits a headerless image but packs the segments
contiguously, which puts .data at the wrong address; mkrawimg.py honours
the vaddr gap so a flat load at 0x80000 lands .text at 0x80000 and .data
at 0x100000, matching the link.)

FAT boot partition
------------------
Format the first SD partition FAT32 and copy onto it:

    bootcode.bin        }
    start.elf           }  Pi firmware (from raspberrypi/firmware, boot/)
    fixup.dat           }
    config.txt          (this directory)
    kernel8.img         (mk kernel8.img)

An easy safe starting point is a spare card written by Raspberry Pi Imager:
leave its firmware files in the FAT boot partition, replace config.txt with
this directory's config.txt, and add kernel8.img.  Do not experiment on a card
whose existing installation or data you need.  The normal raspi3b init opens
the SD block device read-only and reports SD-READ-OK; it does not reformat the
card or run the old destructive driver write test.

config.txt sets arm_64bit=1, kernel=kernel8.img, and routes the PL011
UART (which this kernel's console drives, at 0x3f201000) to the GPIO
header: on a Pi 3 the PL011 is wired to Bluetooth by default, so
dtoverlay=disable-bt hands it back to GPIO 14/15 (header pin 8 TX, pin 10
RX, pin 6 ground), 115200 8N1.  Attach a 3.3 V USB-serial adapter there.

The first serial line names the build ("Inferno/arm64 raspi3b build N"),
and the same N appears later on the "conf raspi3b (N)" line; if it is not
the number the build printed, the card holds a stale image.  This image
also bisects console receive: at nine stages from the firmware's state to
init0 it prints "RXPROBE stage send", polls the PL011 for four seconds for
a key, and prints what arrived with the UART and GPIO pin state.  Press a
key at each prompt.  TICK-OK/TICK-FAIL and SLEEP-OK report whether the
scheduler tick and timed sleeps work.

Exception level
---------------
The firmware enters a 64-bit kernel at EL2 (not EL3).  l.s's core-0 path
reads CurrentEL and drops to EL1 from whichever of EL3 (what QEMU -M
raspi3b gives) or EL2 (real hardware) it finds; an EL3-only drop faults on
the SCR_EL3 write at EL2.

Hardware status
---------------
The SDHOST path gates every FIFO word on the real SDEDM fill level, mailbox
and USB DMA buffers receive explicit cache maintenance, and architectural
CLIDR/CCSIDR-driven set/way invalidation clears every data/unified cache before
SCTLR.C is first enabled.  The kernel is uniprocessor: cores 1-3 are parked at
WFE and MAXMACH is 1.

Physical Pi 3B boots on 13-14 September 2026, with the console on the
PL011 and disable-bt, reached the shell prompt: transmit, the MMU and
caches, the mailbox, the DWC2 root hub and the Dis shell all work on
silicon.  Typed input never arrived (the PL011 receive FIFO stayed empty),
and a kernel process's 10ms timed sleep never returned, so the timer
tick was not waking sleepers either.  One boot with the mini UART as the
console (and no disable-bt) printed nothing at all, so the console stays
on the PL011 while its receive path is bisected.  The EL2 drop now enables
EL1's access to the physical timer (CNTHCTL_EL2), GPU interrupts are
routed to core 0, microdelay() is timed by the counter, and reboot goes
through the PM watchdog instead of PSCI, which a Pi does not have.  The LAN9514/SMSC half of the USB Ethernet driver is implemented
but hardware-unverified, and usbd is not started by the default init.

Emulator boot: the same image
-----------------------------
QEMU and the board boot one file.  qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi3b -kernel
kernel8.img treats the raw image as an arm64 Linux kernel: it loads it at
0x80000, starts core 0 at non-secure EL2 (checked with the monitor's info
registers: PSTATE EL2h) with cores 1-3 waiting in a spin table at 0xd8,
and puts the device tree address in x0 -- what the Pi firmware and its
armstub8 do.  So the EL2 entry path, not an emulator-only EL3 one, is the
one QEMU tests.  qemu.sh runs it; set DTB to the firmware's
bcm2710-rpi-3-b.dtb from the boot partition to hand the kernel the same
tree the card does.  Do not test with -kernel iraspi3b: QEMU loads an ELF
at EL3 with all four cores running, which no Pi ever does.
